## Why This Is Asked
Tests fundamental TDD understanding and ability to follow red-green-refactor cycle - essential for writing maintainable code at Anthropic.

## Expected Answer
Candidate should explain: 1) Write failing test (red), 2) Write minimal code to pass (green), 3) Improve code while tests pass (refactor). Should demonstrate writing test first, then implementation.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Red: Write failing test
test('calculator adds numbers', () => {
  const calc = new Calculator();
  expect(calc.add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
});

// Green: Minimal implementation
class Calculator {
  add(a: number, b: number): number {
    return a + b;
  }
}

// Refactor: Extract if needed
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge cases like negative numbers?
- When would you skip TDD in a real project?
- How do you handle testing async code in TDD?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for feature flags cover a practical risk area: runtime configuration. It invites candidates to reason about defaults, resilience, caching, and cross-environment consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags semantics and defaults
- Client-side vs server-side flag resolution
- Resilience to flag service outages and cache TTL
- End-to-end testing of dynamic UI state

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest example for flag default
import {FlagClient} from './flags';
test('default off when offline', ()=> {
  const fc = new FlagClient({online:false});
  expect(fc.isOn('newHomepage')).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test TTL expiration and cache invalidation?
- How would you simulate a partial outage where some flags are reachable and others are not?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world payments pipelines rely on idempotent, replay-safe processing across multiple services. This question probes practical test design for exactly-once-like semantics in a streaming reconciliation workflow.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency and deduplication in event-driven flows
- Exactly-once-like semantics via transactional writes
- End-to-end replay safety and offset management
- Fault injection and CI validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple idempotent ledger upsert
const processed = new Set();
function applyEvent(evt){
  if(processed.has(evt.id)) return;
  balance += evt.amount;
  processed.add(evt.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate duplicates under clock skew?
- How would you monitor and alert for replayed events?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world serverless reliability and CI/CD integration.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys, conditional writes, DLQ
- Duplicate detection under retries
- End-to-end latency and scale testing
- Local AWS emulation in CI (LocalStack/SAM)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Idea: idempotent Lambda processor sketch
async function handleEvent(e) {
  const id = `${e.key}:${e.version ?? 'latest'}`;
  if (await db.has(id)) return;
  const data = await s3.read(e.bucket, e.key);
  await db.put({ id, data });
}
```

